 fit  that fits the complexity of the real  world  so we talked about model extensibility  flexibility in one way but it also is  extensible and flexible in model  architecture specifically the model  architecture  we can do regressions we can use  advanced architectures like gaussian  processes or auto-aggressive models or  space-based models  an example of this is the profit library  it's one of the most popular  sort of packaged bayesian models that  does a really good job of time series  estimates and  it does this by adding in components for  seasonality in months or days or  whatever you may you whatever may exist  in your demand trend  bayesian models also have the ability to  share data across groups so with the  critical fractal equations you have to  if let's add a newspaper business in new  york in a newspaper  business in los angeles they're not  quite exactly the same but with the  critical fractal equation you can't  treat you can't share any data across  the two cities even though  they're not even though they're kind of  the same even though not exactly the  same  with bayesian models you have this  architecture called multi-level modeling  which lets you share parameters across  different um let's say across different  cities  and in this case you even leverage so  you can leverage small data sets  even more strongly than you could with  other methodologies  and yet another benefit particularly in  industry  is that you can use the human experience  in a principled manner it's hard to go  to executives and say well none of your  experience matters the model told you  this  we haven't considered your experience at  all i tend to  find that if you the people around you  tend to have a lot of experience and if  you can  code that into the model somehow in  invasion models with with priors or in  the model architecture  um it's a it's a quite a good thing to  do  now the argument people make constantly  is that prior is subjective and it ruins  the sanctity of math  but i have to point out that even with  the critical fractal method there's so  much subjectivity  you have to choose a distribution that  you believe is a true distribution that  itself is subjective a lot of times  when you're doing data cleaning you have  to remove outliers or you have to figure  out the time period on which you look  back  both of those choices are subjective so  no matter which  what kind of statistics you use there's  always some sort of subjectivity  um i.e particularly like with bayesian  statistics the subjectivity comes  right at the front and i do want to  point out although we won't go into  priors there are ways to pick priors  that are  that are not subjective um they're  called non-informative priorities so  if you're if this worries you there's  there's a path for you but uh  picking priors invasion statistics is  entirely its own talk  so here's my opinion i think this will  probably cause the most um  consternation or maybe i'll get the most  uh you know  tweets or shout outs on twitter is that  i think the basic community as a whole  also tends to um have a more principled  workflow when it comes to handling  uncertainty in uh in business situations  in my case than uh than the frequency  community i tend to find that when i  read  texts that are like the critical fractal  method it's valid you plug and check  people  force the problem to fit a particular um  derived model  and that's the way the tutorials and  things are written  i find when i joined the bayesian  community  i would instead learn the modeling  workflow and i learned how to build a  model that fit my  um particular situation instead of  learning a bunch of models that i have  to force  upon my particular um situation this is  not to say for question math it's bad or  it's wrong or it's  not right in any way but as a person  that needs to build bespoke models for  bespoke industries like like spacex um  it's good to be able to have the  flexibility to build the type of models  that i need for my particular situation  and so you might ask why are methods  like critical fractals still around  well i mean they're still incredibly  useful um you can see that they're  they're simple and more easily  understood and if you can make the  assumptions  if you have enough data to characterize  the true mean of the two standard  deviation  if things are the same month-to-month  and not changing over time  you should definitely use a critical  fractal equation it is guaranteed to get  you  the the optimum um it's way easier to  implement you saw that it was only  one line of numpy code it wasn't a whole  bunch of models and distributions and  things like that  it's much much simpler to explain to  stakeholders and i would not undervalue  um easy communication to stakeholders i  think  data scientists can get caught up with  building complex models and forget that  they have to explain these complex  models to  people that don't do modeling every day  um and i would say there's a little bit  historical bias i mean the methods have  been around longer because  um the newer bayesian methods are just  newer they've only maybe  mcmc methods and so on have only really  been  been used or or be built into i'll say  quote unquote practical packages for  people like me  in the last you know 20 to 15 years and  critical fractal has been around for  a much greater amount of time  so here's when i suggest you use  bayesian methods though and it's not all  the time  um actually it's quite to be quite  honest it's not  for me i maybe use bayesian methods for  40 30 to 40  of my problems when i was in spacex and  other companies like that  i use it when prior business knowledge  is really important or it's required  like  people know so much that's not in the  data set or it's not in the database  that we need to incorporate their  knowledge somehow  um i do when there's what's called tail  risk when you have to account for all  the possibilities that can happen not  just the one that's the most likely to  happen  and i have to do it when i need a  flexible model architecture that meets  my  my situation and i really do it when  the decision you're making is a really  really big decision um  it does take more work to use the  bayesian methods but some decisions  are make or break they're multi-millions  of dollars they're irreversible  in some particular cases those decisions  can lead to loss of human life  and in those cases you really want to be  taking care that you're using the  methodologies that best fit that  situation and can help you plan for  every possible outcome  and so let me do a quick recap of what  we've done  um we've learned to use data to make  choices even when there isn't a ton of  data  we've learned uh profit and loss  functions and least how to use them in  in the bayesian context um certainly  we've worked with working with  uncertainty  and how posterior predictive posterior  distributions and predict posterior  particular distributions help you work  with uncertainty  and we've shown how to optimize over  posterior predictive distributions  once we have them and the bottom line  again  some decisions are just so important  that you want to be using  the most flexible tools that fit that  situation and in those cases  beijing methods are just in my opinion  the best suited for those types of  decisions  if you're self-learning there's a bunch  of places you can learn  sorry you can read to to get the best  better sense of all the words and things  that i was saying  so we have a lot of docs from the  pioneer three team  but we also have um i'm sorry richard  mcelroth throughout statistical  rethinking there's  spacious method for hackers book that's  free online  and just recently published his  regression and other stories by andrew  gellman  they do a great job of explaining all  the things that i just talked about  if you're an organization though and and  uh you're  looking to just start using basic  statistics immediately  um you can hire us at pioneer labs this  is why we're here we realized that there  are a lot of important decisions being  made  uh in in a sub way if organizations had  a way to get bayesians like myself and  the others that prime c labs involved  now they can  so we're happy to build customized  modeling solutions for your situation we  have tons of experience across  different industries in academia and  most importantly and this is something  we really value it supports the  open source community the package and  and all of it so  um just google us you'll find our  website and  biggest of all i want to thank pi data  global putting on a  conference is uh quite challenging so  thank you volunteers for putting up for  taking up all the uncertainty of uh  planning a conference particularly given  everything going on and making this  happen  i hope you enjoyed this talk thanks  again
 

